    <span class=3D"accordion__heading">December 6, 2022 Senate Runoff Audit=
 Information</span>
  </button>
    <div class=3D"accordion__content-wrapper visually-hidden" id=3D"accordi=
on__content-3" tabindex=3D"-1">
    <div class=3D"accordion__content">
                          <ul>
	<li><a data-entity-substitution=3D"media" data-entity-type=3D"media" data-=
entity-uuid=3D"54278981-0537-46ed-bdb5-0d5bdcdd5d87" href=3D"https://sos.ga=
.gov/sites/default/files/2022-12/Post_Election_Audit_Locations_Dates_and_Ti=
mes-2022-12-13_v3.pdf">December 6, 2022 Runoff Audit Locations and Times</a=
></li>
</ul>

<p><span><span>On December 14-15, 138 Georgia counties counted a random sam=
ple of ballots by hand as part of our post-election audit. We wanted this a=
udit to be as transparent as possible. We could have published the machine-=
counted batch totals so that everyone could have followed along and verifie=
d on their own that the numbers matched as the hand counting occurred. Howe=
ver, if we did that before the audit, some might question whether that gave=
 auditors a =E2=80=9Cnumber to hit.=E2=80=9D</span></span></p>

<p><span><span>Therefore, in order to achieve transparency and audit integr=
ity at the same time, we published a hash of county ballot manifests and ma=
chine counted batch totals:</span></span></p>

<p class=3D"text-align-center"><span><span><strong>238db3862504abfa03873b75=
7356775a56971659b4495dac0119831318a629cb</strong></span></span></p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>Ballot Manifest and Machine Counted Batch Totals</strong></p>

<p><a data-entity-substitution=3D"file" data-entity-type=3D"file" data-enti=
ty-uuid=3D"ce085ebf-8ccd-4644-b6d8-3a83c99f1936" href=3D"https://sos.ga.gov=
/sites/default/files/2022-12/georgia-2022-runoff.zip">CLICK HERE</a> for a =
zip file with all ballot manifests and machine-counted batch totals from th=
e election. A text file labeled =E2=80=9CJurisdictions=E2=80=9D is availabl=
e in the file folder to help identify the county for each file. You can con=
firm that these were the same batch tallies that we started the audit with =
by performing a SHA256 hash of the file and matching it to the hash above, =
which was published before counties began auditing.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>2022-12-15 Press Release:&nbsp;</strong><a href=3D"https://sos.g=
a.gov/news/post-election-audit-senate-runoff-confirms-system-accuracy">Post=
-Election Audit of Senate Runoff Confirms System Accuracy</a></p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
     =20
          </div>
  </div>
</article>
